If the intersection of two coded sets is nonempty, then some index presents the corresponding embedded intersection and the coded intersection.

If the index-set intersection were empty, the emptiness transfer, after removing the whole natural numbers and using that the coded set sits in the master, would make the coded intersection empty. A point of the nonempty index-set intersection lies in both index sets, so the intersection of the embedded neighbourhoods belongs to D, and the membership form of the intersection-index lemma applies.
